  "topic": {
    "id": "precalculus-2-4",
    "courseId": "precalculus",
    "unitId": "precalculus-2",
    "order": 4,
    "code": "2.4",
    "title": "Exponential Function Manipulation",
    "period": "Unit 2",
    "status": "ready",
    "summary": "The product, power, and negative exponent properties, unit-fraction exponents as roots, and how horizontal translations and dilations of exponential functions become vertical dilations and changes of base."
  },
  "framework": null,
  "contentRevision": "fbeec8f4dfc0ca37",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q01",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "product-property",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is equivalent to 2^(x + 4)?",
      "choices": [
        "2ˣ + 16",
        "8(2)ˣ",
        "16(2)ˣ",
        "2^(4x)"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 2,
      "explanation": "By the product property, 2^(x + 4) = 2ˣ·2⁴ = 16(2)ˣ.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "The product property gives a product of powers, not a sum.",
        "8 = 2³, which corresponds to 2^(x + 3).",
        "2^(x + 4) = 2⁴·2ˣ = 16(2)ˣ.",
        "Adding exponents is not the same as multiplying them."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "Exponent 4 contributes the factor 2⁴ = 16.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q02",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "power-property",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is equivalent to 5^(3x)?",
      "choices": [
        "125ˣ",
        "15ˣ",
        "5ˣ + 5ˣ + 5ˣ",
        "3(5)ˣ"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 0,
      "explanation": "By the power property, 5^(3x) = (5³)ˣ = 125ˣ.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "5^(3x) = (5³)ˣ = 125ˣ.",
        "The new base is 5³, not 5·3.",
        "That sum equals 3(5)ˣ, not 5^(3x).",
        "Multiplying the exponent by 3 does not multiply the expression by 3."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "A horizontal dilation by 1/3 cubes the base.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q03",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "negative-and-roots",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "What is the value of 81^(3/4)?",
      "choices": [
        "60.75",
        "243",
        "9",
        "27"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 3,
      "explanation": "81^(3/4) = (81^(1/4))³ = 3³ = 27.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "60.75 = 81(3/4) multiplies by the exponent.",
        "243 = 3⁵ uses the wrong power.",
        "9 is 81^(1/2), the square root.",
        "The fourth root of 81 is 3, and 3³ = 27."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 2,
      "distinguisher": "Take the root from the denominator, then the power from the numerator.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q04",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "negative-and-roots",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is equivalent to (1/4)^(x − 1)?",
      "choices": [
        "4^(1 − x) − 1",
        "4^(1 − x)",
        "4^(x + 1)",
        "−4^(x − 1)"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 1,
      "explanation": "(1/4)^(x − 1) = (4⁻¹)^(x − 1) = 4^(−(x − 1)) = 4^(1 − x).",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "There is no subtracted 1 in the rewritten form.",
        "1/4 = 4⁻¹, and the power property gives 4^(−(x − 1)) = 4^(1 − x).",
        "The negative sign must apply to the whole exponent x − 1, giving 1 − x.",
        "A negative exponent gives a reciprocal, not a negative number."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 2,
      "distinguisher": "Negate the whole exponent: −(x − 1) = 1 − x.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q05",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "product-property",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "The function f is given by f(x) = 3^(x − 2). Which of the following is f written as a vertical dilation of y = 3ˣ?",
      "choices": [
        "f(x) = (1/9)(3)ˣ",
        "f(x) = −6(3)ˣ",
        "f(x) = 9(3)ˣ",
        "f(x) = (1/6)(3)ˣ"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 0,
      "explanation": "3^(x − 2) = 3ˣ·3⁻² = (1/9)3ˣ.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "3⁻² = 1/9, so f(x) = (1/9)(3)ˣ.",
        "3⁻² is 1/9, not 3(−2).",
        "The exponent is −2, so the factor is 3⁻² = 1/9, not 3² = 9.",
        "3² = 9, not 6."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 2,
      "distinguisher": "A negative shift in the exponent gives a factor less than 1.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q06",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "power-property",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is equivalent to 9^(x/2)?",
      "choices": [
        "4.5ˣ",
        "81ˣ",
        "3ˣ",
        "(x/2)⁹"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 2,
      "explanation": "9^(x/2) = (9^(1/2))ˣ = 3ˣ.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "The new base is 9^(1/2) = 3, not 9/2.",
        "81 = 9², which would come from 9^(2x).",
        "The power property gives (9^(1/2))ˣ = 3ˣ.",
        "The base and the exponent are switched."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 0,
      "distinguisher": "Raising to the 1/2 power takes a square root, it does not halve.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q07",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "product-property",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is equivalent to 2^(3x + 2)?",
      "choices": [
        "6ˣ + 4",
        "2(8)ˣ",
        "8ˣ + 4",
        "4(8)ˣ"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 3,
      "explanation": "2^(3x + 2) = 2^(3x)·2² = 4(2³)ˣ = 4(8)ˣ.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "Neither property produces a sum, and 2³ is 8, not 6.",
        "The constant 2 in the exponent gives the factor 2² = 4, not 2.",
        "The product property gives a product 2^(3x)·2², not a sum.",
        "Product property for 2², then power property for 2^(3x) = 8ˣ."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "The added 2 in the exponent becomes the coefficient 2² = 4.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q08",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "negative-and-roots",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is equivalent to (x⁶)^(1/3)·x⁻⁴ for x > 0?",
      "choices": [
        "x⁻¹",
        "x⁻²",
        "x²",
        "x^(−10/3)"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 1,
      "explanation": "(x⁶)^(1/3) = x², and x²·x⁻⁴ = x⁻² = 1/x².",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "x⁻¹ would need (x⁶)^(1/3) to equal x³.",
        "Power property: x⁶·(1/3) = x², then product property: x^(2 − 4) = x⁻².",
        "x² is (x⁶)^(1/3) before multiplying by x⁻⁴.",
        "This adds 6 + 1/3 incorrectly instead of multiplying 6 by 1/3."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 2,
      "distinguisher": "Multiply exponents for a power of a power; add them for a product.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q09",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "power-property",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "describe",
      "prompt": "The graph of g(x) = 4^(x/2) is a horizontal dilation of the graph of f(x) = 4ˣ. Which of the following describes g as a change of base?",
      "choices": [
        "g is f with base 8, because 4·2 = 8",
        "g is f with base 2, because 4/2 = 2",
        "g is f with base 2, because 4^(1/2) = 2",
        "g is f with base 16, because 4² = 16"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 2,
      "explanation": "4^(x/2) = (4^(1/2))ˣ = 2ˣ. A horizontal dilation by a factor of 2 changes the base from 4 to 4^(1/2) = 2.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "The power property raises the base to a power; it does not multiply the base.",
        "The answer 2 is right, but the reason is 4^(1/2) = 2, not 4/2 = 2. For 9^(x/2), 9/2 would give the wrong base.",
        "(4^(1/2))ˣ = 2ˣ.",
        "16 = 4² corresponds to 4^(2x), a horizontal dilation by a factor of 1/2."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "The new base is b^c, a power, not b times or divided by c.",
      "skillLabel": "Describe function characteristics",
      "skillCode": "3.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-4-q10",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-4",
      "concept": "negative-and-roots",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is equivalent to 6^(2x)·6^(−x)?",
      "choices": [
        "6ˣ",
        "6^(−2x²)",
        "36^(−x)",
        "1"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 0,
      "explanation": "6^(2x)·6^(−x) = 6^(2x − x) = 6ˣ.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-4"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "Product property: add the exponents 2x and −x.",
        "Exponents are added for a product, not multiplied.",
        "36^(−x) = 6^(−2x), which reverses the roles of the two factors.",
        "The exponents do not cancel completely: 2x + (−x) = x."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 2,
      "distinguisher": "Add exponents of the same base when multiplying.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    }
  ],
